<td id="S4.T1.3.1.12.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.25pt;padding-bottom:1.25pt;">16.25<sub id="S4.T1.3.1.12.10.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.12.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">±0.47</span></sub></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CHAIR and BLEU scores across LVLMs; lower CHAIR = less hallucination, higher BLEU = better fluency.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate CIPHER on CHAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and Offline POPE (OPOPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> using LLaVA-1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> with Vicuna <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, MiniGPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> with LLaMA2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and mPLUG-Owl2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. For decoding, we use a beam size of 3, setting the maximum number of generated tokens to 64 for CHAIR and 256 for OPOPE. The hallucination subspace is constructed using the top <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> singular vectors, with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=8</annotation></semantics></math> for LLaVA, 64 for MiniGPT-4, and 32 for mPLUG-Owl2, selected via grid search. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, we apply the projection at upper layers (16–32). Visual hallucinations are generated using Stable Diffusion v1.5, with noise added up to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{h}=0.5T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{h}=0.5T</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total number of diffusion steps. We use a classifier-free guidance scale of 7.5. See the Appendix for additional details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Datasets and Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the offline phase, we follow LURE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> using 5,000 image-caption pairs from the MSCOCO training split <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. For evaluation, we follow established evaluation protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and randomly sample 500 images from the MSCOCO validation set using the widely adopted CHAIR and POPE metrics. We report mean and standard deviation over three runs with different random seeds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CHAIR.</span> We use the CHAIR benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> to evaluate object hallucination in generated image descriptions. CHAIR<sub id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">S</span></sub> measures the percentage of sentences that contain at least one hallucinated object, while CHAIR<sub id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I</span></sub> captures the proportion of hallucinated objects among all generated object mentions. We also report BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> to evaluate text quality and confirm that fluency is preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OPOPE.</span> We evaluate object hallucination using the Offline POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which includes three negative sampling strategies: random, popular, and adversarial. We adopt the OPOPE protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which applies a CHAIR-style captioning setup and modifies the evaluation metric to detect the inclusion of negative objects in generated captions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMHAL-Bench.</span>
MMHal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> evaluates LVLMs across eight hallucination types, including attributes, adversarial objects, comparisons, counting, relations, environment, holistic descriptions, and others, providing a broad assessment beyond object-level errors. We measure the response informativeness using GPT-4.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLaVA-Bench.</span> We also evaluate on LLaVA-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, a curated benchmark of 24 diverse images with human-written descriptions and targeted questions. It assesses accuracy and detailedness, complementing CHAIR and POPE. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, we use GPT-4V to compare original and edited responses based on their alignment with the image and question.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare CIPHER with state-of-the-art hallucination mitigation methods, categorized as test-time approaches and post-processing strategies. Test-time methods include DoLa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, VCD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, HALC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, OPERA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and Nullu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. The first four apply decoding-based interventions, while Nullu projects hidden representations onto a hallucination-free subspace. Post-processing methods Woodpecker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and LURE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> detect and rewrite hallucinated spans using external models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:97.9pt;vertical-align:-46.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-60.2pt,11.9pt) scale(0.805051356868271,0.805051356868271) ;">
